/etc/init.d will contain the scripts these scripts can be both sourced / executed.

at the end of each runlevel scripts in /etc/rcn.d will be run. (where n is the run level being entered). Runlevel
S corresponds to bootlevel.

typically you can control the symbolic links between /etc/rcn.d to /etc/init.d using update-rc.d.

for example

update-rc.d -f vmware remove 

this will remove the vmware symbolic links in the different rc.d directories

====================================================================================
RUN LEVELS
====================================================================================
Its a bit different based on distributions but on Debian systems  runlevel 0 is for 
shutdown 1 is single user 2 (default) is multi user/withX 3~5 are the same and 6 is 
for restart (not shutdown)

type runlevel to get current level

telinit `n' will do a change to runlevel `n'

the concept is that of entering a run level. /etc/rc2.d contains [S|K][nn]name scripts where
each of these scripts are symbolic links to /etc/init.d.

S means script will be invoked with start whereas K means it will be invoked with stop. The 
number controls ordering where lower number scripts are invoked before higher numbered ones.
